1.text:
The Tonga flag geometry (solid red field + white canton + centered couped red cross with horizontal arm strictly wider than vertical arm) produces exactly three distinct row patterns in any sufficiently fine rasterization: constant-\(a\) rows outside the canton; rows intersecting only the vertical arm (narrow central \(a\)-block flanked by wider \(b\)-blocks inside canton); and rows intersecting the horizontal arm (wider central \(a\)-block flanked by narrower \(b\)-blocks). These three vectors are linearly independent for \(a\neq b\), so \(\operatorname{rank}(M)=3\).

The repeated rank-2 claims rest on the false premise that the vertical arm equalizes all canton row supports (it does not, once arm widths differ). The four-rectangle indicator \(W\) therefore spans a space of dimension exactly 2, and the pencil \(M=aJ+(b-a)W\) attains rank 3. No rasterization yields a fourth independent pattern.

PUBLIC RESPONSE: {3}

